  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-linear-dependence-and-free-variables">
    <title>Linear Dependence and Free Variables</title>

    <p>
      In light of <xref ref="linindep-matrix-cols"/> and <xref ref="linindep-criterion-inspan">criterion</xref>, it is natural to ask which columns of a matrix are redundant, i.e., which we can remove without affecting the column span.
    </p>

    <theorem xml:id="linindep-pivot-cols">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>v_1,v_2,\ldots,v_k</m> be vectors in <m>\R^n</m>, and consider the matrix
          <md>
<mrow>A = \begin{pmatrix}
| \amp | \amp | \\
v_1 \amp v_2 \amp \cdots \amp v_k \\
| \amp | \amp |
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>.
          Then we can delete the columns of <m>A</m> <em>without</em> pivots (the columns corresponding to the free variables), without changing <m>\Span\{v_1,v_2,\ldots,v_k\}</m>.
        </p>

        <p>The pivot columns are linearly independent, so we cannot delete any more columns without changing the span.</p>
      </statement>

      <proof>
        <p>
          If the matrix is in reduced row echelon form:
          <md>
<mrow>A = \begin{pmatrix}
1 \amp 0 \amp 2 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp 1 \amp 3 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>
          then the column without a pivot is visibly in the span of the pivot columns:
          <md>
<mrow>\begin{pmatrix}
2 \\
3 \\
0
\end{pmatrix} = 2\begin{pmatrix}
1 \\
0 \\
0
\end{pmatrix} + 3\begin{pmatrix}
0 \\
1 \\
0
\end{pmatrix} + 0\begin{pmatrix}
0 \\
0 \\
1
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>,
          and the pivot columns are linearly independent:
          <md>
<mrow>\begin{pmatrix}
0 \\
0 \\
0
\end{pmatrix} = x_1\begin{pmatrix}
1 \\
0 \\
0
\end{pmatrix} + x_2\begin{pmatrix}
0 \\
1 \\
0
\end{pmatrix} + x_4\begin{pmatrix}
0 \\
0 \\
1
\end{pmatrix}
            = \begin{pmatrix}
x_1 \\
x_2 \\
x_4
\end{pmatrix} \implies x_1 = x_2 = x_4 = 0</mrow>
</md>.
        </p>

        <p>
          If the matrix is not in reduced row echelon form, then we row reduce:
          <md>
<mrow>A = \begin{pmatrix}
1 \amp 7 \amp 23 \amp 3 \\
2 \amp 4 \amp 16 \amp 0 \\
-1 \amp -2 \amp -8 \amp 4
\end{pmatrix}
            \quad\xrightarrow{\text{RREF}}\quad\begin{pmatrix}
1 \amp 0 \amp 2 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp 1 \amp 3 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>.
          The following two vector equations have the same solution set, as they come from row-equivalent matrices:
          <md>
<mrow>\spalignsysdelims..\left\{\begin{alignedat}{8}
x_1\begin{pmatrix}
1 \\
2 \\ \amp {}-{} \amp 1
\end{pmatrix} \amp {}+{} \amp x_2\begin{pmatrix}
7 \\
4 \\ \amp {}-{} \amp 2
\end{pmatrix} \amp {}+{} \amp x_3\begin{pmatrix}
23 \\
16 \\ \amp {}-{} \amp 8
\end{pmatrix} \amp {}+{} \amp x_4\begin{pmatrix}
3 \\
0 \\
4
\end{pmatrix} \amp {}={} \amp 0 \amp {} \\
x_1\begin{pmatrix}
1 \\
0 \\
0
\end{pmatrix} \amp {}+{} \amp x_2\begin{pmatrix}
0 \\
1 \\
0
\end{pmatrix} \amp {}+{} \amp x_3\begin{pmatrix}
2 \\
3 \\
0
\end{pmatrix} \amp {}+{} \amp x_4\begin{pmatrix}
0 \\
0 \\
1
\end{pmatrix} \amp {} \amp {} \amp {} \amp {} \amp {} \amp {} \amp {}={} \amp 0 \amp .
\end{alignedat}\right</mrow>
</md>.
          We conclude that
          <md>
<mrow>\begin{pmatrix}
23 \\
16 \\
-8
\end{pmatrix} = 2\begin{pmatrix}
1 \\
2 \\
-1
\end{pmatrix} + 3\begin{pmatrix}
7 \\
4 \\
-2
\end{pmatrix} + 0\begin{pmatrix}
3 \\
0 \\
4
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>
          and that
          <md>
<mrow>x_1\begin{pmatrix}
1 \\
2 \\
-1
\end{pmatrix} + x_2\begin{pmatrix}
7 \\
4 \\
-2
\end{pmatrix} + x_4\begin{pmatrix}
3 \\
0 \\
4
\end{pmatrix} = 0</mrow>
</md>
          has only the trivial solution.
        </p>
      </proof>
    </theorem>

    <p>
      Note that it is necessary to row reduce <m>A</m> to find which are its <xref ref="defn-pivot-pos" text="title">pivot columns</xref>.  However, the span of the columns of the row reduced matrix is generally <em>not</em> equal to the span of the columns of <m>A</m>: one must use the pivot columns of the <em>original</em> matrix.  See <xref ref="dimension-basis-colspace"/> for a restatement of the above theorem.
    </p>

    <example>
      <p>
        The matrix
        <md>
<mrow>A = \begin{pmatrix}
1 \amp 2 \amp 0 \amp -1 \\
-2 \amp -3 \amp 4 \amp 5 \\
2 \amp 4 \amp 0 \amp -2
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>
        has reduced row echelon form
        <md>
<mrow>\begin{pmatrix}
1 \amp 0 \amp -8 \amp -7 \\
0 \amp 1 \amp 4 \amp 3 \\
0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>.
        Therefore, the first two columns of <m>A</m> are the pivot columns, so we can delete the others without changing the span:
        <md>
<mrow>\Span\left\{\begin{pmatrix}
1 \\
-2 \\
2
\end{pmatrix},\;\begin{pmatrix}
2 \\
-3 \\
4
\end{pmatrix}\right\}
          = \Span\left\{\begin{pmatrix}
1 \\
-2 \\
2
\end{pmatrix},\;\begin{pmatrix}
2 \\
-3 \\
4
\end{pmatrix},\;\begin{pmatrix}
0 \\
4 \\
0
\end{pmatrix},\;\begin{pmatrix}
-1 \\
5 \\
-2
\end{pmatrix}\right\}</mrow>
</md>.
        Moreover, the first two columns are linearly independent.
      </p>
    </example>

    <insight xml:id="pivot-cols-dim">
      <title>Pivot Columns and Dimension</title>
      <p>
        Let <m>d</m> be the number of pivot columns in the matrix
        <md>
<mrow>A = \begin{pmatrix}
| \amp | \amp | \\
v_1 \amp v_2 \amp \cdots \amp v_k \\
| \amp | \amp |
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>.
        <ul>
          <li><p>If <m>d=1</m> then <m>\Span\{v_1,v_2,\ldots,v_k\}</m> is a line.</p></li>
          <li><p>If <m>d=2</m> then <m>\Span\{v_1,v_2,\ldots,v_k\}</m> is a plane.</p></li>
          <li><p>If <m>d=3</m> then <m>\Span\{v_1,v_2,\ldots,v_k\}</m> is a 3-space.</p></li>
          <li><p>Et cetera.</p></li>
        </ul>
	The number <m>d</m> is called the dimension.  We discussed this notion in <xref ref="soln-sets-dim-hom"/> and <xref ref="soln-sets-dim-inhom"/>.  We will define this concept rigorously in <xref ref="sec-dimension" text="type-global"/>.
      </p>
    </insight>

  </subsection>

