      <exercise xml:id="exercise-li-15">
        <statement>
          <p><m>
            \setof{\begin{pmatrix}
2 \\
1 \\
3 \\
-1 \\
2
\end{pmatrix},\, \begin{pmatrix}
4 \\
-2 \\
1 \\
3 \\
2
\end{pmatrix},\, \begin{pmatrix}
10 \\
-7 \\
0 \\
10 \\
4
\end{pmatrix}}
          </m></p>
        </statement>
        <solution xml:id="solution-li-15">
          <p><xref provisional="theorem-?"/> says we can answer this question by putting these vectors into a matrix as columns and row-reducing.  Doing this we obtain<md>
<mrow>\begin{pmatrix}
\leading1 \amp 0 \amp -1 \\
0 \amp \leading1 \amp 3 \\
0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp 0 \amp 0
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>.Since the last two columns do not have a pivot, the theorem says the vectors are linearly dependent.</p>
